      "id": "19560",
      "key": "SFCC-935",
      "summary": "Product – Automate Population of First Online Date",
      "description": "Sub-Functional Area:\n Product Data / Merchandising\nDescription\nAs a Site Merchandiser, I want \nfirstOnlineDate\n to be populated automatically when a product first becomes available to purchase, so that the legal record is accurate without manual effort from the merchandising team.\nBackground\nThere is a legal requirement to record the date a product first went live on a storefront. Zimmermann uses this in counterfeit cases.\nToday in Magento this is manual. The content team filters products by the \nbuy\n field (regional launch phase, e.g. \nAU1_NH1\n) and bulk-updates the \ncampaign\n free-text field with the launch date ahead of season launch. The Magento \ncampaign\n attribute is \n[store view]\n scoped, so each storefront holds its own value.\nNot covered by this enhancement — delivered at go-live\nfirstOnlineDate\n is being created as a new SFCC custom attribute as part of the product attribute set (TSD: Product — SFCC Product Attributes, Section 5), migrated from Magento \ncampaign\n. It is manually maintained in Business Manager, replicating current Magento behaviour like-for-like. It is defined as \nsite-specific\n, so each storefront holds its own value, matching Magento's store-view scope today.\nThat covers what the business has today. This enhancement is about removing the manual effort and fixing the accuracy problem.\nWhy automation is needed\nThe manual approach has two known accuracy problems, both flagged by Courtney:\nOnly ~60% of products go live on the planned launch date. Where stock arrives late, the actual date is later, so the recorded date is wrong — and wrong in the direction that weakens a counterfeit claim.\nThe value is entered ahead of launch, so it records intent rather than fact.\nThe correct date is an observed event: the first moment a product is simultaneously online and available above the stock buffer, \non that specific storefront\n. No single field can produce it, and the answer differs per site — a product may go live in AU weeks before the US.\nProposed approach\nA scheduled SFCC job that runs \nper site\n and stamps \nfirstOnlineDate\n the first time a product is both online (online flag true, \nonlineFrom\n passed or blank) and available above the configured buffer for that site. Once set for a given site, the value is never overwritten. Blank means the product has not yet been live on that storefront.\nAll inputs already exist: online flag and \nonlineFrom\n (TSD Section 3), stock with buffer (SFCC-707), and per-site product assignment via site catalogs. No MuleSoft or AP21 change is required.\nNotes\nFunctionality does not currently exist in Magento — the manual process is the current state\nDepends on SFCC-707 (Buffer Stock in SFCC) for the availability input\nRelated: TSD: Product — SFCC Product Attributes, open item #10 and Section 5 (\nfirstOnlineDate\n)\nOpen questions for refinement\nPrecision — day-level, or does the legal requirement need a timestamp? Day-level allows a daily job; timestamp precision requires hourly or event-driven.\nShould the attribute become read-only in BM once automated? A legal record a merchandiser can overwrite is weak evidence.\nBehaviour when a product goes offline and returns — first date retained, which is the intent, but worth confirming with legal.\nBackfill of migrated manual values — retained as-is, or flagged as unverified?\nWhether the job stamps all sites a product is assigned to, or only those where it has actually met the online-and-available condition. The latter is correct for the legal record but means a product carries different dates per storefront.",
